  "worked_solution": "The function is continuous at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=0$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$ $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\nOn the other hand, the function has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(1)=1$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)\\ne f(1)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, II is true while I and III are false.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
